Detect stateless resets after a path change or behind another connection's ID

Applies to picoquic a602e60babea48da7849169d716aaf68684e66d6, after 0002,
0006, 0007, 0008, 0009 and 0010.

RFC 9000 Section 10.3.1 has an endpoint compare a datagram's last 16 bytes
with every stateless reset token associated with the remote address it
arrived from, for the connection IDs in use, whenever the first packet cannot
be associated with a connection or cannot be decrypted, and compare without
leaking the token's value. Upstream falls short in three ways, each pinned by
a test here:

- The (address, token) pair a connection registers for that comparison is set
  at the ready transition and renewed only with a connection ID rotation, not
  when the default path's first tuple changes. After a NAT rebinding the
  server sends to the new address with a new client-issued connection ID, and
  a reset from that address carrying that ID's token is not recognized.
  picoquic_register_net_secret_for_path re-registers the pair whenever the
  default path's first tuple changes: a tuple promoted on NAT rebinding
  (picoquic_set_first_tuple), one promoted once its challenge is verified, a
  first tuple whose challenge failed, and a first tuple deleted while the path
  lives. The pair in use is registered; the previous tuple's pair is not kept
  while its own challenge runs.
- A packet associated with a connection by its leading bytes but not
  decryptable was compared with that connection's token only, so a reset for
  connection B whose leading bytes happen to name connection A was missed. The
  comparison now falls through to the pairs registered for the remote
  address, and the connection it names is the one reset.
- That comparison used memcmp, and the registered-pair comparison was constant
  time only on Apple and 32-bit Windows. Both use
  picoquic_constant_time_memcmp on every platform.

picoquic_set_stateless_reset_sending(quic, 0) makes a context never answer an
unassociated packet with a stateless reset (Section 10.3, a MAY) while it
keeps receiving and detecting them: an application running several contexts
on one port leaves the answer to the context that owns the connection.
picoquic_set_default_stateless_reset_min_interval remains a rate limit and
cannot disable sending.

Tests (picoquic_ct): stateless_reset_after_rebinding,
stateless_reset_other_cnx_prefix and stateless_reset_sending_disabled fail
without the corrections, each at the assertion it names; the existing
stateless_reset, nat_rebinding and migration tests still pass. LibMoQ's
compare/picoquic-reset reproducer reports neither finding against a picoquic
built with this patch.

Upstream picoquic/packet.c, quicctx.c, paths.c and frames.c are unchanged in
the lines this patch touches since the pin as of 2026-10-19 (master
5fc4330fdab47458a596f95bfc757fab284f7882); the patch applies to that master.

